{:tier :glm-5.2, :verse 24, :comments ["<b>That which proceeds from your lips you shall keep and do.</b> By way of the plain sense (peshat): this returns to the matter of the vow, for He had warned with a negative prohibition (לא תאחר) and had not mentioned a positive commandment, and it would be fitting for it to say: 'When you vow a vow, you shall do it as you vowed and not delay to fulfill it.' Therefore it says: 'that which proceeds from your lips' — that is, the vow — 'you shall keep and do,' as you vowed to HaShem your Elohim with the freewill offering of your heart, 'and as you have spoken with your mouth' — that is, let no word fall to the ground of all that your heart agreed to and your lips uttered. And all this He said because of the severity of the vow, for thus did He intensify its punishment, saying: 'and it shall be a sin in you' — that is, the sin shall be fixed in your soul, in the manner of what is said (Numbers 15): ״הנפש ההיא עונה בה״, and likewise concerning the poor man it is written (Deuteronomy 15): ״וקרא עליך אל ה' והיה בך חטא״, for withholding a laborer's wage, which is his very soul — the sin becomes fixed in his soul, a soul for a soul; and the same applies to this punishment, for the soul is the life of the body, and it is measure for measure: because he delays the vow, which is the life of the King; therefore it says of it: ״כי דרוש ידרשנו ה' אלהיך״ — this is the King Himself, who watches over particulars, and He is the One who demands retribution (דורש דמים); He will demand it of him in this world and in the world to come, and this is the redoubling of the language of demanding (ידרשנו)." "<b>And by way of</b> the Midrash: 'That which proceeds from your lips you shall keep and do.' 'That which proceeds from your lips' — this is a positive commandment. 'You shall keep' — this is a negative commandment. 'And do' — an admonition to the court, that they make you do it. That is, 'and you shall do' — even against your will. If they are vows of the altar such as offerings, they compel him until he says 'I am willing,' as is said (Leviticus 1): ״יקריב אותו לרצונו״. And if in the matter of charity, all the more so do they compel him and place a lien (וממשכנין). And thus did the Sages of blessed memory expound: they place liens for charity even on the eve of Shabbat. 'As you vowed' — this is a vow. 'To HaShem your Elohim' — these are sin-offerings and guilt-offerings, burnt-offerings and peace-offerings, concerning which is written (Deuteronomy 12): ״ה' אלהיך״. 'A freewill offering' — as its plain meaning. 'That which you have spoken' — these are donations for the Temple treasury (קדשי בדק הבית). 'With your mouth' — this is charity, on account of what is said (Isaiah 45): ״יצא מפי צדקה דבר ולא ישוב״. Thus did the Sages of blessed memory expound at the beginning of [the chapter on] Rosh HaShanah." "<b>And by way of</b> the Kabbalah: 'that which proceeds from your lips' corresponds to 'the mouth of HaShem.' And this is the obligation imposed upon a person to fulfill everything that proceeds from his mouth, even without an oath at all. And the Sages of blessed memory said: 'No, no — twice is an oath'; for the Holy One, blessed be He, said it at the Flood, in the verse (Genesis 8): ״וירח ה' את ריח הניחח״, and it is written (Isaiah 54): ״אשר נשבעתי מעבור מי נח עוד על הארץ״. But a single utterance is not an oath, but rather an obligation to fulfill one's word. From this they said: whoever goes back on his word — the One who exacts punishment (מי שפרע) takes it upon Himself. And furthermore, we are children of the Omnipresent, and we have been sanctified with the covenant of the mouth, which is aligned in the center between the ten fingers of the hands, and with the covenant of circumcision between the ten fingers of the feet. And behold, the mouth is the seventh among the orifices of the head, which are seven: two ears, two eyes, two nostrils, and the mouth is the seventh. And the Holy One, blessed be He, chose the seventh: He created seven heavens and chose the seventh, which is Aravot; He created seven days and chose Shabbat; and likewise He chose the mouth to praise Him and to declare His praise, for it is the instrument with which His holy name is praised, and this is (Leviticus 22): ״ולא תחללו את שם קדשי״ — an admonition not to profane the covenant of the mouth, which is made to praise His holy name. And likewise it is not fitting for us to profane our lips or to lie in any one of the covenants, in the manner of what Scripture says (Psalms 44): ״ולא שקרנו בבריתך״." "<b>And behold,</b> Israel — when they would transgress that which proceeds from His mouth, Moses said to them (Numbers 14:41): ״למה זה אתם עוברים את פי ה'״. Therefore it says: 'that which proceeds from your lips you shall keep' — that is, you are obligated to fulfill what proceeds from your lips; for 'keep' — this is 'that which proceeds from your lips you shall keep.' And there is no need to say regarding the commandments — this is 'you shall keep and do': 'you shall keep' is a negative commandment, and 'you shall do' is a positive commandment. And there is no need to say regarding the vow, which is above the commandments — and this is 'as you vowed to HaShem your Elohim.' 'A freewill offering' (נדבה) — the freewill offering emanates from the Name and is the culmination of the structure (סוף הבנין), and it is the elevation-offering (תרומה), in the manner of what is written (Exodus 35:5): ״כל נדיב לבו יביאה את תרומת ה'״ — below. And it is that we heard the utterances from His mouth — this is 'that which you have spoken with your mouth,' and it is voice (קול) and spirit (רוח) and speech (דבור), and this is the spirit of prophecy (רוח הקודש). And concerning this they expounded: 'that which you have spoken' — these are donations for the Temple treasury (קדשי בדק הבית)."], :book :deuteronomy, :voice :rabbeinu-bahya, :lang :en, :chapter 23, :notes {}, :source-witnesses [{"versionTitle" "Sefaria community Hebrew (PD)", "language" "he"}], :timestamp "2026-07-30T13:51:00.441822365Z", :translated-from :he, :model "glm-5.2"}